`@Service` and `@Repository` are both `@Component` under the hood, but they add layer semantics (and, for `@Repository`, exception translation).

```java
// This works but is architecturally unclear
@Component
public class OrderProcessor {
    private final OrderValidator validator;
    private final OrderDataHandler dataHandler;

    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        validator.validate(order);
        dataHandler.save(order);
    }
}

// Better: semantic clarity and AOP targeting
@Service // Enables transactional pointcuts, communicates intent
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderValidator validator;
    private final OrderRepository repository;

    @Transactional // AOP advice can target all @Service beans
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        validator.validate(order);
        repository.save(order); // @Repository enables exception translation
    }
}

@Repository // Exception translation active
public class OrderRepository {
    // DuplicateKeyException thrown if order ID exists
}
```

```java
@Controller // returns view names by default
class UserApi {
    @GetMapping("/users")
    @ResponseBody // needed on EVERY method that returns data
    List<User> all() { return service.findAll(); }
}
```

```java
@RestController // = @Controller + @ResponseBody on the class
class UserApi {
    @GetMapping("/users")
    List<User> all() { return service.findAll(); } // serialized to JSON
}
```
Use `@Controller` for server-rendered views and `@RestController` for REST APIs that return data.

The **@Component** annotation tells Spring Boot's component scanning mechanism to detect and register a class as a **bean** in the application context.

@Component is the catch-all stereotype—the generic marker. When Spring starts, it scans your packages for @Component-annotated classes, instantiates them, resolves their dependencies, and makes them available for injection elsewhere. While Spring offers specialized stereotypes like **@Service** (business logic), **@Repository** (data access), and **@Controller** (web layer), @Component is the generic fallback for classes that don't fit these architectural layers. Use it for infrastructure concerns like **event listeners** that react to application events, **scheduled tasks** that run periodically, **validators** that enforce business rules, **converters** that transform data types, or **configuration helpers** that wire up other beans.

The key principle: if a class needs Spring's dependency injection but doesn't belong to service/repository/controller layers, it's a component.

```java
@Component
public class OrderEventListener {
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    public OrderEventListener(NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    @EventListener  // Spring detects this because class is a @Component
    public void handleOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        notificationService.sendConfirmation(event.getOrderId());
    }
}

@Component
public class DataInitializer implements ApplicationRunner {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    public DataInitializer(UserRepository userRepository) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
    }

    @Override
    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) {
        // Runs once at startup, needs dependency injection
        if (userRepository.count() == 0) {
            userRepository.save(new User("admin", "admin@example.com"));
        }
    }
}
```

```java
// No annotation: Spring never creates this class as a bean,
// so any @Autowired EmailSender fails to start the context
public class EmailSender {
    public void send(String to, String body) {
        // ... talks to an SMTP server ...
    }
}
```

```java
@Component // detected during scanning, available for injection
public class EmailSender {
    public void send(String to, String body) {
        // ... talks to an SMTP server ...
    }
}
```
`EmailSender` isn't a service, repository, or controller—it's a plain infrastructure helper, so `@Component` is the correct stereotype.

```java
@Repository
public class UserRepository {
    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

    public UserRepository(JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate) {
        this.jdbcTemplate = jdbcTemplate;
    }

    public void save(User user) {
        // If email already exists, throws SQLException with code 23505 (unique violation)
        jdbcTemplate.update(
            "INSERT INTO users (email, name) VALUES (?, ?)",
            user.email(), user.name()
        );
    }
}

@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repository;

    @Transactional
    public void registerUser(User user) {
        try {
            repository.save(user); // Repository throws vendor-specific exception
        } catch (DuplicateKeyException e) { // Service catches Spring's common exception
            throw new BusinessException("Email already registered");
        }
    }
}
```

```java
@Component // no translation: a raw vendor error escapes the data layer
public class UserDao {
    public void save(User u) {
        // a duplicate email throws java.sql.SQLException directly,
        // forcing the service layer to import JDBC types
    }
}
```

```java
@Repository // Spring's proxy converts the vendor error for you
public class UserDao {
    public void save(User u) {
        // a duplicate email surfaces as Spring's
        // DataIntegrityViolationException — no JDBC types in the service
    }
}
```
The service layer now catches `DataIntegrityViolationException` and never references a vendor exception class.

